# The **reverse direction**: the GST records carry an invoice the taxpayer's
# own register never recorded — Plan 108 Slice 4.
#
# **No other rule in this pack runs this way round, and that is the gap it
# closes.** Every finding before it starts from the register and asks what the
# authority says about it, which can only ever find problems with invoices the
# taxpayer already knows about. An invoice the books have never heard of is
# invisible to all of them — and it is the case where a CA finds *unclaimed
# credit* rather than an over-claim, so it is the one finding here that can be
# worth money rather than cost it.
#
# **This is a bookkeeping-completeness question, not an eligibility one**, which
# is why it cites `gst:Section16` — the general eligibility section, whose
# condition is that the recipient holds the invoice — rather than
# `Section16(2)(aa)`, which is about the *supplier* having furnished details.
# Here the supplier plainly has.
#
# **Surfaced, never written.** There is a real temptation to "add it to the
# books", and it is the wrong instinct: a GSTR-2B line filed against this
# GSTIN by mistake would then become a purchase this taxpayer appears to have
# made. The finding asks the question; a human answers it.
#
# The join is anchored on the declared side and the register side is the
# `OPTIONAL` — the mirror image of `missing-in-gstr2b.sparql`, with the same
# `OPTIONAL` + `!BOUND` idiom and the same `GRAPH ?var { }` discipline, both
# documented there.
#
# **The two sides are joined through a shared canonical `gst:Invoice`
# subject — Plan 109 Slice 2** — `?canonical gst:recordedIn ?booked` replaces
# what used to be a shared `?key`+`?gstin` binding.

PREFIX gst: <https://graph-owl.dev/packs/gst#>

SELECT ?declared ?number ?gstin ?taxableValue ?taxAmount ?invoiceDate
WHERE {
  GRAPH ?gstr1 {
    ?canonical gst:appearsIn ?declared .
    ?declared a gst:Gstr1Invoice ;
              gst:issuedBy ?declaredSupplier ;
              gst:invoiceNumber ?number ;
              gst:invoiceKey    ?key ;
              gst:invoiceDate   ?invoiceDate ;
              gst:taxableValue  ?taxableValue ;
              gst:taxAmount     ?taxAmount .
    ?declaredSupplier gst:supplierGstin ?gstin .
  }

  OPTIONAL {
    GRAPH ?register {
      ?canonical gst:recordedIn ?booked .
      ?booked a gst:PurchaseInvoice .
    }
  }

  # **The same keying-error guard `missing-in-gstr1.sparql` carries, mirrored
  # — and, like that one, deliberately still `?key`-based rather than
  # canonical-subject-based, for the identical reason: it must match *any*
  # GSTIN, and the canonical subject is computed from the GSTIN.**
  # — and found the same way, against real data. A GSTIN transposed between
  # the register and the filing makes the exact join above fail in *both*
  # directions, so one typo produced "the supplier never filed it" and "you
  # never booked it" about the same invoice, both false, alongside the
  # transposition finding that was already saying the true thing.
  #
  # Matching on invoice number and taxable value under any GSTIN: the claim
  # "the register does not carry this invoice" is falsified by such a row
  # existing, whoever it is booked against. Its cost is written up in the
  # other query and applies identically here.
  OPTIONAL {
    GRAPH ?anyRegister {
      ?bookedUnderAnotherGstin a gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
              gst:invoiceKey    ?key ;
              gst:taxableValue  ?taxableValue .
    }
  }

  FILTER (!BOUND(?booked))
  FILTER (!BOUND(?bookedUnderAnotherGstin))
}
ORDER BY ?number
